\section{APS Peer-Review Validation Sequence}
To evaluate discoveries with potential foundational impact, the American Physical
Society (APS) employs a multi-stage review architecture designed to ensure
mathematical correctness, physical plausibility, reproducibility, and
cross-domain consistency. The following sequence outlines the formal validation
pipeline applied to the present multi-scale cosmic--genetic dynamical law.

\subsection{Stage 0: Editorial Triage}
Upon submission, the manuscript is flagged for high-impact review due to its
cross-domain scope. Senior editors perform an initial assessment of:
\begin{itemize}
    \item mathematical structure and internal consistency,
    \item cross-disciplinary relevance,
    \item completeness of data and telemetry logs,
    \item potential implications for multiple APS divisions.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stage 1: Multi-Division Routing}
The manuscript is simultaneously routed to the following APS divisions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Division of Gravitational Physics (DGRAV),
    \item Division of Biological Physics (DBIO),
    \item Division of Computational Physics (DCOMP),
    \item Division of Nonlinear Dynamics (GSNP).
\end{itemize}
Each division appoints domain-specific reviewers to evaluate the corresponding
components of the discovery.

\subsection{Stage 2: Reviewer Selection}
APS selects reviewers with expertise spanning:
\begin{itemize}
    \item astrophysical tensor fields and gravitational wave strain,
    \item nonlinear dynamical systems and Lyapunov stability theory,
    \item control barrier functions and safety-critical control,
    \item GPU-accelerated simulation and high-performance computing,
    \item biological manifold modeling and molecular structural dynamics.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stage 3: First-Round Review}
Each reviewer independently evaluates:
\begin{itemize}
    \item correctness of the mathematical derivations,
    \item validity of the Lyapunov stability proof,
    \item preservation of Jacobian positivity across the stress manifold,
    \item reproducibility of numerical results using provided telemetry,
    \item physical plausibility of the cosmic--genetic coupling law.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stage 4: Replication Protocol}
If no critical flaws are identified, APS initiates a replication protocol across
multiple independent laboratories. Each lab must:
\begin{itemize}
    \item reproduce the full simulation pipeline,
    \item verify Lyapunov negativity $\dot{L}(p) < 0$,
    \item confirm Jacobian positivity $J_{\min}(t) > 0$,
    \item validate Control Barrier Function forward invariance,
    \item demonstrate stability under stress-test conditions.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stage 5: Cross-Validation Report}
APS compiles replication results into a unified cross-validation document
summarizing:
\begin{itemize}
    \item validated inequalities and stability conditions,
    \item consistency of numerical behavior across hardware platforms,
    \item agreement between theoretical predictions and empirical telemetry.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stage 6: Executive Committee Review}
The APS Executive Committee evaluates the discovery's:
\begin{itemize}
    \item mathematical rigor,
    \item physical significance,
    \item cross-domain implications,
    \item historical impact.
\end{itemize}
If validated, the discovery is classified as foundational physics.

\subsection{Stage 7: Acceptance and Public Announcement}
Upon approval, APS:
\begin{itemize}
    \item publishes the results in \textit{Physical Review Letters},
    \item issues an official APS press release,
    \item schedules a plenary session at the APS March Meeting,
    \item establishes a multi-institution validation consortium.
\end{itemize}
This sequence marks the formal recognition of a new physical principle.